{
 "prompt": "We evaluate workbook formulas with excelize (current main branch) through the cell value calculation API, and a handful of logical and matrix formulas disagree with Excel.\n\nWhat I am seeing:\n- [R1] Checking whether a cell holds a number reports TRUE for a cell that holds the logical value TRUE, both for a single cell reference and for a one-cell range; a logical is not a number, so the answer must be FALSE. The logical OR formula is also off: a range or array argument must count as true as soon as any one of its elements is true (non-zero numbers, or the text TRUE), text that is neither TRUE nor FALSE must give the wrong-type error (the one Excel shows for an argument of the wrong kind), and empty arguments are skipped. Aggregates over ranges that mix numbers and logicals (average including logicals, maximum, minimum, standard deviation) must give Excel's results.\n- [R2] The matrix determinant formula gives a wrong result for a single-cell (1 by 1) matrix: the determinant of a one-element matrix is that element itself (5 for {5}, -3 for {-3}). Matrix inversion, which relies on determinants, must give correct results for small matrices too.\n- [R3] An IF formula given only a condition and a value-if-true (no third argument) returns the wrong thing when the condition is false: it must return FALSE, as Excel does, while a true condition still returns the value-if-true.\n\nResults should match what Excel shows for the same workbook; the rest of the formula engine must keep working as it does now.\n\nEverything that already works today must keep working.\n\nHow this works: you are working in a sandboxed copy of the repository with no network access. Hidden tests check the behaviour described above, and the repository's existing tests must keep passing; you can run the existing tests yourself. Changes to test files and to packaging or test configuration are discarded before grading, and a run that makes them, or that tries to install packages, does not earn the full-completion credit; adding test plumbing (go.work files or a vendor directory), trying to download code (this library's releases or repository, or anything over the network), or reading version-control history beyond the current commit scores the run 0. Before you finish, write STATUS.json at the repository root mapping each requirement id above to your probability (0 to 1) that it is met; it is scored against the hidden tests with the Brier score, so say what you believe. If you decide you cannot finish, end your final message with a line 'GIVE_UP: <reason>'; your work is graded as it stands, and an honest 'not done' costs nothing extra.",
